Hence $c_{1}=-c_{2} / 200 \omega^{2}$. Now, we use the initial conditions to solve for $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$. Since $x(0)=50$, we have $c_{1}=50$. Hence $c_{2}=50 \cdot 200 \omega^{2}$. Thus $x(t) = 50 \cos \omega t + 50 \cdot 200 \omega^{2} \sin \omega t$. Next, we find the particular solution to the nonhomogeneous equation $x^{\prime\prime}(t)+\frac{1}{200} x(t)=\frac{1}{8} \sin t$ by assuming that $x(t)=A \sin t+B \cos t$. Then $x^{\prime}(t)=A \cos t-B \sin t$ and $x^{\prime\prime}(t)=-A \sin t-B \cos t$. Substituting these into the nonhomogeneous equation gives
